update lr_explanation_choice_notes t set body=v.nb from (values
('b7a65957-2e87-44e7-a774-b4de9da7ce21'::uuid,'a86c27af00aa04c9c0efb794d55e298d','The economist covers two cases: spending goes up significantly, or spending goes down significantly. Both lead to lower unemployment. But nothing says one of those two things will happen. Spending could stay about the same, and then neither rule applies.

This answer closes that gap. If spending will either rise or fall significantly, one of the two cases must happen, and either way unemployment decreases. The conclusion follows. On the next either-way argument, check whether the cases listed are really the only options, and look for the answer that makes them the only options.'),
('3edb70f9-f304-41d1-a775-ab6b64837d6c'::uuid,'04cbbea7be0fcf25b869b37f9e09b25f','D talks about the long run, while the conclusion is that unemployment will soon decrease. Even if the economy slows years from now, that tells us nothing about next year. And D still doesn''t say whether spending will significantly go up or down, so the middle case stays open.'),
('74abf194-80aa-4ac4-b219-d502bca5f8fc'::uuid,'ece29ca6689599516611c5b2822b899b','E sounds connected because it uses both mechanisms from the premises. But diagram it: unemployment decreases → economy stimulated or businesses retain earnings. That tells us what is required for unemployment to fall, not that either requirement will be met. A rule that can only lead to "will not decrease" can never prove that unemployment will decrease.'),
('9ade4d67-f775-47e3-8471-517a0b140a36'::uuid,'fe94a400e8b0bfd465e93d7d901f71f8','A is about who takes care of the pet, and it never says what any child should or should not have. A sufficient assumption has to deliver the conclusion''s new idea, "should not have pets." Combine A with the premises and you still can''t say a word about what children should do.'),
('d11a7cb4-e3de-4e02-931a-2da00d648b8f'::uuid,'457be08bc2a4d1ade5ecd29ace2040be','Diagram it: understand → should be allowed to have pets. Our under-sixes mostly don''t understand, so B''s trigger never fires for them. B is the reversal of the rule we need (should have pets → understand). Knowing that children who understand may have pets says nothing about whether children who don''t understand may have them too.'),
('18eeb4f8-c8c6-4dde-9ad0-b2b3e1d12dad'::uuid,'e5ee16fd75d7721e6d3b6b14cbca4bf5','C says what is the case: most egocentric and selfish children "do not have pets." The conclusion is about what should be the case. A fact about who currently owns pets can''t prove that anyone ought not to own one. No "should" goes in, so no "should" comes out.'),
('255e69f0-195e-4c1d-8288-cf4a80f565a8'::uuid,'0dfdf5ac1dd59c381a6d92221ee03045','D is tempting because it sounds like the premises, but that is the problem: it repeats the evidence (egocentric, doesn''t understand) and adds nothing about what children should do. Stacking more evidence of the same kind never closes a gap to a "should" conclusion. Ask of every answer here: does it contain "should not have pets" or its contrapositive?'),
('73f300eb-dde0-40cf-9825-c0551a684316'::uuid,'cc298e757dcbd1a39c645eb50ba4faea','"The only children who should have pets are those who understand that their pets are independent creatures with their own feelings and needs." A sentence of the form The only X are Y means X → Y, so: should have pet → understand. Contrapositive: not understand → should not have pet.

Now chain it with the evidence. Most children under six → not understand → should not have pet. So most children under six should not have pets, which is the conclusion.

The move to repeat: find the new concept in the conclusion ("should not have pets"), then look for the rule that puts it on the right side of an arrow whose left side is something the evidence gives you.'),
('8614b418-d011-48c3-a40c-31c9ae37ef8f'::uuid,'19d382c95d1b2c013a02e7c9e126a7b8','Diagram A: speaking quickly with little repetition → gesturing. That connects speech and gesturing again, which the evidence already did, just in the other direction. It never mentions finding phrases, and a sufficient assumption has to bring the conclusion''s new idea in. With A added, we still can''t conclude anything about phrases.'),
('1095c381-be8c-4e0b-a62d-0dae646e7505'::uuid,'31b11b06e1d1f46b096b27a40633132e','B is about how the cartoons were chosen. At most, that makes the experiment look fairer. But a fairer experiment still only measured speed and repetition. B says nothing about finding phrases, so it can''t make the conclusion follow.'),
('4efb2500-3b16-4358-aef4-235164d28667'::uuid,'e88b0fc4c889300bb185f9c10860cb40','C is the bridge we predicted. Its trigger, "Any form of behavior correlated with quicker speech and less repetition in speech," is exactly what the experiment showed about gesturing. Its outcome, helping speakers "find the phrases they want quickly," is exactly the conclusion.

Chain it: gesturing → correlated with quicker speech and less repetition → helps find phrases. Done.

Notice how low C sets the bar. It only asks for a correlation, which is all the experiment delivered. When the evidence is a study and the conclusion names something the study never measured, the sufficient assumption is usually a rule that turns what was measured into what was concluded.'),
('cd62b45d-1d5f-4c79-a194-fec46ab7545b'::uuid,'0e50a2e4d0feb0a996a6c866a76b9daa','D is the trap. Diagram it: helps find phrases → speak more quickly and repeat less. We need the opposite direction: quicker speech and less repetition → helps find phrases.

Here''s why the direction matters. D tells us that anything that helps you find phrases will speed you up. It does not tell us that anything that speeds you up is helping you find phrases. Gesturing could speed people up for some other reason, like the animation idea in the Evaluate step, and D would still be true. D starts from the thing we are trying to prove, so it can never prove it.'),
('0dbc2c0b-3cc0-43e5-a17c-7a616abbc59f'::uuid,'3e68acda6fbcd5fff54df2ab36449520','E says that within the gesturing group, the fastest, least repetitive speakers gestured the most. That makes the link between gesturing and fluent speech more convincing. But it is still only about speed and repetition. Making the evidence stronger doesn''t close the gap to finding phrases; on Sufficient Assumption, only a rule that reaches the conclusion''s idea can do that.'),
('df92f509-18c5-488e-8e3f-79691fa31640'::uuid,'999722ab745b429df0f090e0acf32873','"No children who spend their free time reading stories fail to develop a lifelong interest in literature." Cut the double negative: reads stories → lifelong interest. That makes reading enough for the interest, but we need it to be required. Test it: even if every child reader develops the interest, the many non-readers could develop it too, so the number is never capped at "few."'),
('097d09d0-0d0f-4c7a-b7ca-f7b268399f05'::uuid,'88ca3b6a157ac6497c8e069b75b296b4','"Only those people who currently spend their free time reading stories will develop a lifelong interest in literature." "Only" marks the right side of the arrow: lifelong interest → currently reads stories. Contrapositive: doesn''t read now → no lifelong interest.

Now the count works. Everyone who will develop the interest has to come from the reading group. Few children are in that group, so few children will develop the interest.

The move to repeat: to prove that few (or no) people will have X, make X require something few (or no) people have.'),
('caf2e5e2-9c98-40d1-a49e-fd22f76ac9f5'::uuid,'ae38f94a93f71382df01f1471e82c322','C diagrams as: no TV at home → reads stories. That''s about reading and television only. It never mentions a lifelong interest in literature, so no combination of C and the evidence can reach the conclusion.'),
('1d44775a-4e98-421f-964c-d6b6286654de'::uuid,'3f7a8476f0f9bf9fb0a014f9235b2c30','D is tempting because it finally mentions a lifelong interest in literature. But its trigger is watching "a great deal of television," and the evidence never says children do that. It says TV is everywhere and few children read. Without knowing most children are heavy watchers, D never fires, and it says nothing about children who don''t read but don''t watch much TV either.'),
('205203ef-a168-4cc4-86b2-d955dbac0a00'::uuid,'16b837771146733b22e29861c8400876','E tells us that few child readers watch television. Like C, it stays inside reading and TV and never touches a lifelong interest in literature. An answer without the conclusion''s new idea can''t make the conclusion follow.'),
('d9cdebe3-e0a4-46d4-9924-66392367a670'::uuid,'20fb5d559d7ae3ba3b5e6b2fd70f2261','Diagram A: decisions unaffected by profit → absolute integrity. That''s the illegal negation of what we need (affected by profit → not absolute integrity). A only tells us about films free of profit pressure, and most indie films aren''t, so A''s trigger never fires for them. A is perfectly consistent with a profit-affected film having absolute integrity.'),
('1f0b6a17-b66b-4526-b766-5a501bc4ba50'::uuid,'e6afba85ced8fdf84d9e5a09dd47f77a','B is the rule we predicted. Its trigger, "If any of the artistic decisions made in creating something were affected by the need to make profits," matches the evidence about most indie films. Its outcome, "does not have absolute integrity as a work of art," is the conclusion.

Chain it: most indie films → profit-affected decisions → not absolute integrity. So most indie films lack absolute integrity.

Notice the word "any." It makes the trigger easy to hit: one profit-affected decision is enough. A sufficient assumption wants a trigger the evidence clearly satisfies and an outcome that is exactly the conclusion.'),
('97ab4149-56ab-4bc9-9e10-e57956186e0e'::uuid,'d9df454191b32cbb8ecf0c7c28454ec9','C compares individuals with groups "on average." The filmmaker never sorts filmmakers into individuals and groups, and a comparison of averages can''t prove that any particular film lacks absolute integrity. C never connects profit to integrity, which is the gap.'),
('84f4327d-461a-45c8-8385-ed8094e5a009'::uuid,'70221ef8b3289fa4335405a1ab4384c7','D says unconventionality has "no bearing" on integrity. That undercuts the view in the first sentence, but knocking out one factor doesn''t tell us what does destroy integrity. D gives us no rule that ends in "do not have absolute integrity," so the conclusion still doesn''t follow.'),
('7cb66323-c5d0-4c32-8b53-6ff70928c676'::uuid,'c32c82385ed6fa55fee41dd07b0a7c9e','E is the tempting one because it has the shape of a correct answer: "no integrity ... unless the artistic decisions made in creating it were unaffected." But read to the end: unaffected "by views about what is conventional," not by the need to make profits. The evidence says profit affected the decisions. It never says views about conventionality did. So E''s trigger is never met, and it can''t tell us anything about indie films. Always check that the trigger matches the evidence word for word in meaning.'),
('bcf50a2d-7d9a-4b78-83bb-da8006520c9a'::uuid,'579e61fb76341a60c7b67aa3b8c3e940','Diagram A: not acidic → beneficial. That''s the illegal negation of the link we need (acidic → not beneficial). A tells us about ponds without acidic water, and Wallakim''s water is acidic, so A''s trigger never fires. A is perfectly consistent with acidic water also being beneficial to turtles.'),
('57d19364-0c3e-4182-8ae9-a44db39ecc23'::uuid,'8c56cb267b468725625c8554ed7e0fec','Calling acidity the "most important factor" says acidity matters, but not which way. It never says acidic water is bad for turtles. Even if it were, other good conditions might still make Wallakim beneficial overall. We need a guarantee that acidic means not beneficial, and B doesn''t give one.'),
('1a37c9a0-14c0-4614-8029-10fe06c8a2d0'::uuid,'de76cf76769cadaf6fb81523615f2b6a','C only ranks the two ponds. Wallakim''s conditions could be less beneficial than Sosachi''s and still be beneficial enough for turtles to thrive. A comparison can''t tell us whether Wallakim clears the bar, and the conclusion is about Wallakim alone.'),
('473f7832-f974-40dc-80a4-8650bb84212a'::uuid,'1dc30fff8ff60a371a052ca2e1bd3830','Diagram D: Wallakim not acidic → thriving. Its contrapositive is: not thriving → acidic. Neither version starts from "acidic," which is the fact we have. Test it: Wallakim could be thriving right now with acidic water, and it could also thrive if the acid were gone. D allows both, so it can''t prove Wallakim isn''t thriving.'),
('8a0f1ceb-b244-4a4e-9e20-1c92afae5245'::uuid,'a12b8c4e61c7197244c9afd9caf5611b','"The conditions at a pond are beneficial to turtles only if the water in the pond is not acidic." The words only if introduce the right side: beneficial → not acidic. Contrapositive: acidic → not beneficial. That''s our missing link.

Chain it with the evidence: Wallakim → acidic → not beneficial → not thriving. The conclusion follows.

The move to repeat: when the link you predicted doesn''t appear in the choices, check for its contrapositive, and remember that "only if" points to the necessary side.'),
('6d046934-9a9e-4840-a4ac-7cc5d163f169'::uuid,'0e0545656d3befd977e6b424695e3da9','A puts the non-programmer members of B17 in the Hanson Building, which is the wrong subgroup. Say B17 has 100 members: 70 programmers and 30 non-programmers. A puts about 20 of the 30 non-programmers in Hanson. All 70 programmers could still work elsewhere, so no programmer is guaranteed to be in the building.'),
('1525687a-700f-4618-8a6e-6a222632e3d5'::uuid,'621ea693d04eda21e07597617b8a3fa8','The executive committee never appears in the argument, and nothing says its members are programmers. Test it: the committee could be made up entirely of B17''s non-programmers. Then most of the committee works in Hanson and still no programmer does.'),
('3dcb7988-0fd6-45b5-850b-9c57db05d75d'::uuid,'4b9697ff02a3fd7973981ac2937e7da4','C is the tempting one because it links B17 and the Hanson Building, just in the wrong direction. C says "most of the government employees who work in the Hanson Building are members of Bargaining Unit Number 17." That is a "most" about Hanson workers, not about B17 members, so it doesn''t pair with our premise.

Run numbers. B17 has 1,000 members: 700 programmers and 300 non-programmers. Hanson has 20 workers, and 15 of them are B17 non-programmers. Most Hanson workers are B17 members, and still no programmer works there. The two "most" statements must be about the same group to force an overlap.'),
('1710fbd7-b36e-4232-8d7a-d05ad61378cb'::uuid,'a3e0bee64630d7b424f37369e4156173','D gives the second "most" about the same group. More than half of B17 are programmers (premise), and more than half of B17 work in the Hanson Building (D). Two groups that each take up more than half of B17 have to overlap, so at least one member is both a programmer and a Hanson worker. That person is a government employee who works in the Hanson Building and is a computer programmer, which is the conclusion.

The move to repeat: when a "most" premise has to produce a "some" conclusion, look for a second "most" about the same group.'),
('ce21e629-1fbd-4fe3-ab8e-4618f26f4bd0'::uuid,'3312d1e23967258d7f590fdbb9ce8dfe','E tells us most Hanson workers are government employees, but it never connects the building to B17 or to programmers. Government employees in Hanson could all be from other units with no programmers at all. With no tie to B17, there''s no overlap to force.'),
('289a4c15-33eb-490e-8a63-246409dd1bb3'::uuid,'bd25114853c6b6f7e31a4ffffaddff98','A survey the Association only plans to run next year gives us no data now. Announcing a survey isn''t evidence of anything, and it doesn''t touch the Survey Group''s 20 years of flat sales. Even if the new survey comes out differently, A doesn''t tell us that.'),
('68183fc3-4093-4c1f-9ba6-751848661e58'::uuid,'2d3d4d0c7459e3d7bb303176f063bece','Being "generally receptive to the idea of trying new types of tea" is an attitude, not a purchase, and it isn''t a change over time. The public could have been just as receptive 20 years ago. Barr''s conclusion is about tea being more popular now than before, and B gives no before-and-after comparison.'),
('c0fb0987-0d69-4438-a800-f3233a7b7e7d'::uuid,'9fd745c49da7ccb7d048446b3c4ddd1d','C is trying to make us doubt the Survey Group. But why would consumer advocacy groups want tea sales to look flat? C gives no motive, and the stimulus calls the Survey Group neutral. Funding alone, with no reason to skew the data, doesn''t weaken the survey.'),
('132f3d4a-b8a5-4d21-94ed-1c6dea6bb1a7'::uuid,'6f416528afcfc8cec6b41d82bd9878f1','This is the excuse for the survey data we predicted. If every surveyed store is "located in the same small region of the country," flat sales there tell us very little about the country as a whole. Tea could be booming everywhere else.

The move to repeat: when the evidence is a survey or a sample, weaken by showing the sample doesn''t represent the group the conclusion is about.'),
('8fa4ece0-ad52-4651-a9ac-1a8a959d82e4'::uuid,'39d90770d37cd11a15b5fa18a0b6f820','E is the tempting one because an "expensive and efficient advertising campaign" sounds like it should boost popularity. But it tells us about effort, not results, and Barr''s data says sales didn''t move. If anything, E helps Barr: it explains the visibility the Association cited as paid advertising, so that visibility is weaker evidence that people actually like tea more.')
) v(id,ob,nb) where t.id=v.id and md5(t.body)=v.ob returning t.id;